; Sample journal — demonstrates the plain-text accounting format
; supported by SwiftLedger.

; Account declarations are optional. They let you explicitly set the
; account type when the name root doesn't match the recognised conventions.
account Assets:Checking
account Assets:Savings
account Assets:Brokerage
account Assets:PettyCash
account Assets:EuroAccount
account Assets:PoundAccount
account Liabilities:CreditCard
account Equity:Opening
account Income:Salary
account Income:Freelance
account Expenses:Rent
account Expenses:Food:Groceries
account Expenses:Food:Restaurants
account Expenses:Transport
account Expenses:Utilities

; ── January ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

2024-01-01 * Opening balances
    Assets:Checking        $2500.00
    Assets:Savings         $5000.00
    Equity:Opening

2024-01-15 * Salary
    Assets:Checking        $3200.00
    Income:Salary

2024-01-01 * Rent — January
    Expenses:Rent          $1200.00
    Assets:Checking

2024-01-07 Groceries
    Expenses:Food:Groceries    $87.45
    Liabilities:CreditCard

2024-01-12 Lunch with client
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants  $42.00
    Liabilities:CreditCard

2024-01-20 Monthly transit pass
    Expenses:Transport         $95.00
    Assets:Checking

2024-01-22 Electricity bill
    Expenses:Utilities         $68.30
    Assets:Checking

2024-01-28 Pay off credit card
    Liabilities:CreditCard     $129.45
    Assets:Checking

; ── February ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

2024-02-01 * Rent — February
    Expenses:Rent          $1200.00
    Assets:Checking

2024-02-05 Supermarket run
    Expenses:Food:Groceries    $112.60
    Liabilities:CreditCard

2024-02-10 Freelance project payment
    Assets:Checking        $800.00
    Income:Freelance

2024-02-15 * Salary
    Assets:Checking        $3200.00
    Income:Salary

2024-02-18 ! Pending refund  ; status: pending
    Assets:Checking        $35.00
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants

2024-02-20 Bus and train fares
    Expenses:Transport         $42.50
    Assets:Checking

2024-02-25 Internet and phone
    Expenses:Utilities         $55.00
    Assets:Checking

2024-02-28 Pay off credit card
    Liabilities:CreditCard     $112.60
    Assets:Checking

; ── Entries with fewer than two postings ────────────────────────────────────
; The rule is that every commodity nets to zero, not that there are two
; postings, so these are valid in ledger, in hledger and here.

; A dated line on its own is how a journal keeps a note.
2024-02-03 Called the bank about the card fee

; A single posting of zero touches an account without moving money.
2024-02-06 Opened a brokerage account
    Assets:Brokerage       $0

; With nothing to balance against, an elided amount reads as zero, exactly
; as if 0 had been written.
2024-02-08 Set up the petty cash tin
    Assets:PettyCash

; The usual hledger shape: a zero posting carrying a balance assertion, which
; states the balance on that date without recording a movement. SwiftLedger
; preserves the assertion and does not check it.
2024-02-29 Statement check
    Assets:Savings         $0 = $5000.00

; ── Virtual postings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; Parentheses make a posting virtual: it moves money in a tracking account
; outside the double-entry books and takes no part in balancing. Square
; brackets make it balanced virtual — exempt from balancing against the real
; postings, but the bracketed postings of one entry must sum to zero among
; themselves. Both names are stored bare, so a query finds the same account
; however the journal spells it.

2024-03-01 Groceries, and move the food envelope
    Expenses:Food:Groceries             $60.00
    Assets:Checking
    [Assets:Checking:Envelope:Food]     -$60.00
    [Assets:Checking:Available]         $60.00
    (Reserve:Capital)                   $250.00

; ── Several commodities ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; Each commodity balances on its own, and one elided posting absorbs the
; remainder of every commodity in the entry, which is how ledger and hledger
; read it. The equity line below is read as two postings, -€1.500,00 and
; -£400.00, though the file keeps the one line as written. The euro account is
; written the way a European statement writes it, with a comma dividing the
; fraction and a point grouping the thousands.

2024-03-05 * Opening balances for the accounts abroad
    Assets:EuroAccount     €1.500,00
    Assets:PoundAccount    £400.00
    Equity:Opening

; A commodity the written postings already net to zero leaves nothing to
; absorb, so the elided line gets no posting in it: here only -£25.00.
2024-03-09 Lunch and a train abroad
    Expenses:Transport     €30,00
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants  £25.00
    Assets:EuroAccount     -€30,00
    Assets:PoundAccount

; ── An exchange with no price written ───────────────────────────────────────
; An entry written in two commodities with no price on either line is read as
; an exchange: the euros cost the dollars, at the rate the two amounts imply.
; ledger and hledger both balance it that way. Nothing is written into the
; file for it, and no report converts anything: the rate is a reading of the
; entry, there for anyone who asks the transaction what it balances by.

2024-03-18 Bought euros at the bureau
    Assets:EuroAccount     €100,00
    Assets:Checking       -$110.00

; ── A price on a posting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; A posting can say what its amount cost in another commodity: @ writes the
; price of one unit, @@ the cost of the whole posting. The entry balances on
; that cost rather than on the face value, which is how the two lines below
; net to zero although no two of their amounts share a commodity. A total
; cost keeps its own sign and takes the posting's on top of it, so the same
; line written -€30,00 @@ $32.70 would cost -$32.70.

2024-03-15 Dinner in Vienna, paid with the card
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants  €30,00 @ $1.09
    Liabilities:CreditCard     -$32.70

; ── Decimal marks and a comment after an amount ─────────────────────────────
; Which mark divides the fraction is read out of the number: a mark with
; anything other than three digits after it divides, so €12,50 is twelve euros
; fifty, as most of Europe writes it, and the €1.500,00 in the opening entry
; above is fifteen hundred. hledger and ledger 3 both read €12,50 that way, so
; the entry below means the same in either tool. A mark with exactly three
; digits after it is the one form the number cannot settle, and it keeps
; ledger's reading, where $1,000 is a thousand and $1.000 is one; hledger reads
; $1,000 as one unless a directive declares the commodity. An entry SwiftLedger
; rebuilds is written back with the marks the file uses for that commodity, and
; this file uses both: an edited euro entry comes back €12,50 while an edited
; dollar entry comes back $1200.00, each in the style its own amounts are
; written in.
; A ; after an amount opens the posting's comment even with one space in front
; of it, as it does in ledger and in hledger, since two spaces are what end an
; account name and the name has ended by then.

2024-03-12 Coffee in Vienna
    Expenses:Food:Restaurants  €12,50 ; one space is enough to start a comment
    Assets:EuroAccount     -€12,50

; ── Comment blocks ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; A `comment` line on its own opens a block that runs to `end comment`, or
; to the end of the file. Nothing inside it is read: the entry below is
; parked, not booked, so it touches no balance and appears in no report,
; and the file is written back with the block exactly as it was.

comment
Draft of the March rent, not yet due. Everything in here is text.
2024-03-01 * Rent for March
    Expenses:Rent          $1200.00
    Assets:Checking
end comment

% Lines starting with % or | are full-line comments too, as in ledger
| and hledger. So is a line starting with *, the org-mode heading.
